      "seed": 6364,
      "response": "To determine the number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made while ensuring that a shirt and a tie are not of the same color, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of colors and items:**\n   There are 8 colors, and for each color, there is one shirt and one tie.\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of possible shirt-and-tie combinations without any restrictions:**\n   Since there are 8 colors, and each color has a unique shirt and tie, there are \\(8 \\times 8 = 64\\) possible shirt-and-tie combinations.\n\n3. **Identify the number of combinations where the shirt and tie are of the same color:**\n   For each color, there is exactly one combination where the shirt and tie are of the same color. Since there are 8 colors, there are 8 such combinations.\n\n4. **Calculate the number of valid shirt-and-tie combinations:**\n   To find the number of valid shirt-and-tie combinations where the shirt and tie are not of the same color, we subtract the number of invalid combinations from the total number of combinations.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of valid combinations} = \\text{Total combinations} - \\text{Invalid combinations}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of valid combinations} = 64 - 8 = 56\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of shirt-and-tie outfits that can be made, ensuring that a shirt and a tie are not of the same color, is \\(\\boxed{56}\\).",
